#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069949 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to plot the curve $y=x^2$. We start by making a table of values.\n\n| **$x$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y=x^2$ | $9$ | $4$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n\nWe can now plot the graph using the $(x,y)$ values in our table.\n\n![](images/57/576148f61e683e58c508b212221c103d9ef9914f549afabfec92a04ec778a268.png)\n\nThis curve's shape is known as a **parabola**, and the **minimum point** at $(0,0)$ is known as the **vertex** of the parabola." :tutorial/id #uuid "98e8af90-38c4-57f6-a494-cc5214d10c9b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1633 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069952 :tutorial/content "Horizontal and vertical translations can be combined. It does not matter which we do first.\n\nIn general, the graph of\n\n$$\ny=(x-h)^2+k\n$$\n\nis a translation of the standard parabola $y=x^2$ by\n\n- $h$ units right, and\n- $k$ units up.\n\nThe vertex of\n\n$$\ny=(x-h)^2+k\n$$\n\nis located at $(h,k)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "b17f43fa-0d9e-5d24-b900-72a6dececdad" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1853 :tutorial/title "Combinations of Horizontal and Vertical Translations"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069953 :tutorial/content "The graph of $y=(x-3)^2$ looks like the standard parabola $y=x^2$, but shifted $3$ units to the *right* along the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/a8/a88222e0210fcd33cf3147044a14e66ce491a4ae5660640c6c1856ef8dc2025d.png)\n\nSimilarly, the graph of\n\n$$\ny=(x+2)^2\n$$\n\nlooks like the standard $y=x^2$ parabola after it's been shifted $2$ units to the *left*:\n\n![](images/1a/1a28e319cc19e1fdb5a378ed95f5d87f6ecdfbcf1bb6a26b76776b23203ede00.png)\n\nIn general, the graph of $y=(x-h)^2$ looks like the standard parabola $y=x^2$ but translated $h$ units right. The vertex of $y=(x-h)^2$ is located at $(h,0)$.\n\n**Watch Out!** It's easy to confuse the translations.\n\n- For $y=(x-3)^2$, we subtract $3$ from $x$ inside the parentheses, and the parabola moves to the right, *not* to the left (even though it seems like it should be the other way around).\n- Likewise, for $y=(x+2)^2$, we add $2$ to $x$ inside the parentheses, and the parabola moves to the left, *not* to the right." :tutorial/id #uuid "5e424d1b-91e1-5860-b0c9-5ca519476af7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1854 :tutorial/title "Horizontal Translations of a Parabola"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069980 :tutorial/content "Adding a value to $y = x^2$ will **translate** (shift) the parabola up or down the $y$-axis. For instance, adding $\\color{blue}2$ to $y = x^2$ will move each point on the parabola $2$ units up.\n\n![](images/54/54d1dec8f7f4af1345c7f6814e0847a6a3543a5d09800c260f05f285ea3b1112.png)\n\nOn the other hand, subtracting $\\color{red}3$ will move each point of the parabola $3$ units down.\n\n![](images/c2/c245072fcd86c4ba6617cf1c4bde76ef8aa0a9edfc3b740ce8b0270fec711bb6.png)\n\nIn general, the graph of\n\n$$\ny=x^2+k\n$$\n\nlooks like the standard parabola $y=x^2$ but translated $k$ units up. The vertex of\n\n$$\ny=x^2+k\n$$\n\nis located at $(0,k)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "82fdbf6d-9723-58e6-ba17-37a663d4fe1c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4230 :tutorial/title "Vertical Translations of a Parabola"}]}
